<Sequence DisplayName="2. Mode switch" sap2010:WorkflowViewState.IdRef="Sequence_40">
<sap2010:Annotation.AnnotationText>One project, two entry modes:
  SWEEP  started by the 15-minute time trigger (staggered :07 :22 :37 :52)
  QUEUE  started by a queue trigger on Conflicts
Community Edition gives one unattended robot, so the stagger keeps this job from
colliding with the Reminder Bot's :00 and :30 runs.</sap2010:Annotation.AnnotationText>
<ui:Comment Text='TODO(Studio): If in_Mode = "SWEEP" run the sweep sequence, else run the queue sequence' sap2010:WorkflowViewState.IdRef="Comment_39" />
<Sequence DisplayName="SWEEP mode - detect and enqueue" sap2010:WorkflowViewState.IdRef="Sequence_6">
<sap2010:Annotation.AnnotationText>Runs on the 15-minute trigger. It DETECTS AND ENQUEUES; it never resolves.

Some conflicts announce themselves (a doctor marked unavailable). Others are
silent — a time overlap from regenerated slots, a row missing from Excel. The
sweep finds the silent ones and pushes them into the same Conflicts queue, so
every conflict goes through one resolution path and one audit trail.

It also re-enqueues every OPEN conflict. The HMS pushes new conflicts as they
are raised, but a push can fail; the queue enforces unique references
(CFL-{conflict_id}), so re-enqueueing is a no-op for items already queued and a
recovery for any that were lost.

Double-bookings come in two kinds, both returned by the detect endpoint:
  SAME_SLOT     two appointments on one slot row — impossible through the normal
                path because of the partial unique index; checked anyway
  TIME_OVERLAP  same doctor, overlapping times, different slot rows — the kind
                that actually happens and no index can prevent</sap2010:Annotation.AnnotationText>
